TOMBSTONE 01 ~~> THE VALIDATOR THAT REFUSED A VALID MOVE
═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
WHAT IT WAS
  i13valid() folded the token stream RIGHT-TO-LEFT, checking
  "net < arity" before consuming. Postfix operands precede their
  operator, so a right-to-left fold sees the operator first and
  refuses a well-formed move.
HOW IT DIED
  Run before the page. R's valid move [A0,A0,B2] came back REFUSED.
  The KNOWN-GOOD failed — same class of fault as a known-bad passing.
  If R's own moves are refused, the throne rejects the honest player.
THE FIX
  Fold LEFT-TO-RIGHT as a stack depth: atom (arity 0) pushes +1,
  a node of arity k pops k and pushes 1 -> net delta (1-k). Valid
  iff depth never goes negative and ends at exactly 1.
LESSON
  Every instrument needs its known-GOOD tested too, not just its
  known-bad. A throne that refuses the honest move looks identical
  to a throne working — until the honest player is turned away.
